## Get Group Last Values

> HTTP Request
> <div class="http"><span class="method-get">GET</span><code class="path">/api/v2/{username}/groups/{group_key}/data/last_value</code></div>

```shell
# CSV lines of feed_key,value for every feed in the group
$ curl -H "X-AIO-Key: {io_key}" https://io.adafruit.com/api/v2/{username}/groups/{group_key}/data/last_value

# JSON object of feed key to most recent value
$ curl -H "X-AIO-Key: {io_key}" "https://io.adafruit.com/api/v2/{username}/groups/{group_key}/data/last_value?type=json"
```

> Response Sample (no `type` or `type=csv`, `text/plain`):

```text
temperature,22.5
humidity,48
```

> Response Sample (`type=json`):

```json
{
  "feeds": {
    "temperature": "22.5",
    "humidity": "48"
  }
}
```

Returns the most recent value of every feed in the group in one request. Feeds are keyed as `group_key.feed_key` when the group is not your default group. Feeds with no data yet have an empty value in CSV and `null` in JSON. Requires an `X-AIO-Key` and read access to the group.

### Path Parameters

Parameter | Type    | Required  | Description
--------- | ------- | --------- | -----------------------
username  | string  | true      | a valid username string
group_key | string  | true      | a valid group key

### Query Parameters

Parameter | Type    | Required  | Description
--------- | ------- | --------- | -----------------------
type      | string  | false     | `json` returns a JSON object; anything else (including `csv`) returns `feed_key,value` CSV lines.


## Group-Scoped Feed Endpoints

> HTTP Request
> <div class="http"><span class="method-get">GET</span><code class="path">/api/v2/{username}/groups/{group_key}/feeds/{feed_key}</code></div>
> <div class="http"><span class="method-put">PUT</span><code class="path">/api/v2/{username}/groups/{group_key}/feeds/{feed_key}</code></div>
> <div class="http"><span class="method-delete">DELETE</span><code class="path">/api/v2/{username}/groups/{group_key}/feeds/{feed_key}</code></div>
> <div class="http"><span class="method-get">GET</span><code class="path">/api/v2/{username}/groups/{group_key}/feeds/{feed_key}/details</code></div>

```shell
# get a feed within a group
$ curl -H "X-AIO-Key: {io_key}" https://io.adafruit.com/api/v2/{username}/groups/{group_key}/feeds/{feed_key}

# update a feed within a group
$ curl -X PUT -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d '{"feed": {"description": "Living room"}}' -H "X-AIO-Key: {io_key}" https://io.adafruit.com/api/v2/{username}/groups/{group_key}/feeds/{feed_key}

# delete the feed
$ curl -X DELETE -H "X-AIO-Key: {io_key}" https://io.adafruit.com/api/v2/{username}/groups/{group_key}/feeds/{feed_key}
```

A feed can be reached through its group as well as directly. These endpoints behave exactly like <a href="#get-feed">Get Feed</a>, <a href="#update-feed">Update Feed</a>, <a href="#delete-feed">Delete Feed</a> and <a href="#get-feed-details">Get Feed Details</a>, with the same parameters and responses; the only difference is that the feed key in the response is scoped to the group. Updating and deleting require that you own the feed. Note that `DELETE` deletes the feed itself (and its data), not just its membership in the group; use <a href="#remove-feed-from-group">Remove Feed from Group</a> to only take it out of the group.

The feed's data is available the same way, and behaves exactly like the <a href="#data">Data</a> endpoints for a feed:

Method | Path
------ | ----
GET, POST | `/api/v2/{username}/groups/{group_key}/feeds/{feed_key}/data`
POST | `/api/v2/{username}/groups/{group_key}/feeds/{feed_key}/data/batch`
GET, PUT, PATCH, DELETE | `/api/v2/{username}/groups/{group_key}/feeds/{feed_key}/data/{id}`

Reading a public group works without a key, see <a href="#public-feeds">Public Feeds</a>.

